////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// fft_accelerator - AI verification, prepared 11/15/26
// Following manual_verify.mv style; signed at Marc103's explicit request.
// Sign-off tags applied at Marc103's explicit instruction on 11/15/26.
// Reviewed current fft_accelerator.sv, its package helpers, the reader/writer,
// both FFT arithmetic cores and the file-driven accelerator testbench.
// Source excerpts are verbatim. Rising-edge descriptions refer to values after
// nonblocking assignments settle unless input sampling is stated explicitly.
// Current RTL SHA-256: d843d30c5fc0aee67e12d12e05411ec8e7206a897732acbf6d39a4696fc11afc

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 01. Scope and data representation
- This wrapper connects fsm_fft_reader -> fft_multiplier_core -> fft_adder_core
  -> fsm_fft_writer. Sample and twiddle memories remain external.
- Each component is a 1+EXPONENT_WIDTH+MANTISSA_WIDTH-bit ABFP word; complex
  memory words are {real, imaginary}. Sign/exponent/mantissa bits are preserved.
- ABFP32 here means 6 exponent bits and 25 explicit mantissa bits, not IEEE32.
  BASE remains independent of these field widths; the supplied files use BASE=16.
- Exponent-zero values must have zero mantissa. Finite nonzero values have a
  nonzero leading radix digit. Exponent all ones plus mantissa MSB one is the
  existing combined NaN/Inf class. The wrapper does not reinterpret these rules.
- Arithmetic remains separate rounded ABFP operations, not a fused butterfly.
- Iterative radix-2 DIT needs bit-reversed inputs for natural-order FFT outputs.
  There is no permutation stage, output normalization or 1/N scaling here.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 02. Public parameters and widths
// Source: fft_accelerator.sv
"
module fft_accelerator #(
    parameter BASE,
    parameter EXPONENT_WIDTH,
    parameter MANTISSA_WIDTH,
    parameter SAMPLES_ADDR_WIDTH,
    parameter TWIDDLES_ADDR_WIDTH,
    parameter II_CYCLES = 4,
    parameter READ_LATENCY = 1,
    parameter WRITE_LATENCY = 1,
    parameter RESET_PIPELINE_DEPTH = 1,
    parameter LUTX = 6,
    parameter GRADE = 1,
    parameter REGSITERED_MULTIPLIER = 1,
    parameter REGISTERED_NORMALIZE = 1,
    parameter REGISTERED_SWAP_IN = 1,
    parameter REGISTERED_SWAP_MUX = 1,
    parameter REGISTERED_ALIGN_STICKY_IN = 1,
    parameter REGISTERED_ALIGN_EXPONENT_MUX = 1,
    parameter REGISTERED_ALIGN_MUX = 1,
    parameter REGISTERED_ADD_CONVERT = 1,
    parameter REGISTERED_ADD_CONVERT_CONVERT = 1,
    parameter REGISTERED_PRIORITY_ENCODE = 1,
    parameter REGISTERED_PRIORITY_ENCODE_ENCODER = 1,
    parameter REGISTERED_NORMALIZE_ROUND_POST_NORMALIZE = 1,
    parameter REGISTERED_NORMALIZE_ROUND_POST_NORMALIZE_ROUND = 1,
"
- Required format/address parameters are BASE, EXPONENT_WIDTH, MANTISSA_WIDTH,
  SAMPLES_ADDR_WIDTH and TWIDDLES_ADDR_WIDTH. Register flags retain child meanings.
- REGSITERED_MULTIPLIER preserves the spelling of the established child option.
- DATA_WIDTH derives from the arithmetic format. COMPLEX_DATA_WIDTH is twice
  that width, so sample and twiddle interfaces have identical complex layouts.
- Width/latency localparams call globally defined helpers using public parameters;
  changing a register flag changes the arithmetic and companion delays together.
- Reader contracts require SAMPLES_ADDR_WIDTH=1..30, TWIDDLES_ADDR_WIDTH>=1,
  II_CYCLES>=4 and READ_LATENCY>=1. WRITE_LATENCY>=1 and reset depth>=0.
- The scaffold TB deliberately limits address widths to <=15/14 for its fixed
  32768-sample/16384-twiddle memories. This is a TB limit, not a new RTL limit.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 03. Derived complex width and reset hold
// Source: ../../rtl_utilities_pkg.sv
"
    function automatic int fft_accelerator_COMPLEX_DATA_WIDTH(int EXPONENT_WIDTH, int MANTISSA_WIDTH);
        return 2 * (1 + EXPONENT_WIDTH + MANTISSA_WIDTH);
    endfunction

    function automatic int fft_accelerator_RESET_HOLD_CYCLES(int RESET_PIPELINE_DEPTH, int WRITE_LATENCY);
        return max(RESET_PIPELINE_DEPTH + 2, WRITE_LATENCY);
    endfunction
"
- A complex word has two complete ABFP fields; neither sign bit is discarded.
- RESET_HOLD_CYCLES covers reset propagation into the reader's extra input
  register and outstanding fixed-latency external write stages.
- These helpers use ordinary int values; actual buses are sized in the modules.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 04. Dedicated reset distribution
// Source: fft_accelerator.sv
"
    // Dedicated equal-depth reset pipelines for each child and wrapper controls.
    logic reader_rst_p, multiplier_rst_p, adder_rst_p, writer_rst_p;
    logic control_rst_p, reset_active;
    pipe #(.DATA_WIDTH(1), .LATENCY(RESET_PIPELINE_DEPTH)) reader_reset_pipe (
        .clk_i(clk_i), .data_i(rst_i), .data_o(reader_rst_p)
    );
    pipe #(.DATA_WIDTH(1), .LATENCY(RESET_PIPELINE_DEPTH)) multiplier_reset_pipe (
        .clk_i(clk_i), .data_i(rst_i), .data_o(multiplier_rst_p)
    );
    pipe #(.DATA_WIDTH(1), .LATENCY(RESET_PIPELINE_DEPTH)) adder_reset_pipe (
        .clk_i(clk_i), .data_i(rst_i), .data_o(adder_rst_p)
    );
    pipe #(.DATA_WIDTH(1), .LATENCY(RESET_PIPELINE_DEPTH)) writer_reset_pipe (
        .clk_i(clk_i), .data_i(rst_i), .data_o(writer_rst_p)
    );
    pipe #(.DATA_WIDTH(1), .LATENCY(RESET_PIPELINE_DEPTH)) control_reset_pipe (
        .clk_i(clk_i), .data_i(rst_i), .data_o(control_rst_p)
    );
    assign reset_active = rst_i || control_rst_p;
"
- Reader, multiplier, adder and writer each receive their own reset pipe output.
  A fifth pipe supplies wrapper control reset. All start directly from rst_i and
  have the same RESET_PIPELINE_DEPTH; delays are not cascaded between modules.
- At depth zero each pipe bypasses combinationally. Positive depth uses the
  existing unreset pipe registers, so startup requires the documented reset hold.
- reset_active combines raw reset with the wrapper-control delayed reset. Raw
  assertion suppresses external traffic immediately; delayed reset holds masking
  through propagation. Each child still resets its synchronous controls locally.
- No data or address register is cleared by these distribution pipes.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 05. Reader connection and launch gating
// Source: fft_accelerator.sv
"
    assign reader_go = go_i && !busy_o && !reset_active;
    assign samples_rd_addr_o = read_addr;
    assign samples_rd_en_o = sample_read_enable && !reset_active;
    assign twiddles_rd_en_o = twiddle_read_enable && !reset_active;
    fsm_fft_reader #(
        .DATA_WIDTH(COMPLEX_DATA_WIDTH), .SAMPLES_ADDR_WIDTH(SAMPLES_ADDR_WIDTH),
        .TWIDDLES_ADDR_WIDTH(TWIDDLES_ADDR_WIDTH), .II_CYCLES(II_CYCLES), .READ_LATENCY(READ_LATENCY)
    ) reader (
        .clk_i(clk_i), .rst_i(reader_rst_p), .go_i(reader_go),
        .fft_point_size_i(fft_point_size_i), .fft_whole_iterations_i(fft_whole_iterations_i),
        .a_b_rd_addr_o(read_addr), .a_b_rd_en_o(sample_read_enable),
        .a_valid_o(a_valid), .b_valid_o(b_valid),
        .w_rd_addr_o(twiddles_rd_addr_o), .w_rd_en_o(twiddle_read_enable), .w_valid_o(w_valid),
        .busy_o(reader_busy), .fft_done_o(reader_done)
    );
"
- Runtime N and whole-FFT count M pass to the reader, which registers its inputs.
  Require power-of-two N>=2 and positive M with M*N within sample capacity.
- The reader generates B and W together, followed by A one clock later. Its
  request interval remains II_CYCLES across pair/group/whole/stage boundaries.
- go_i is gated while top-level busy/reset is active. Busy-time pulses are not
  queued. A level held high through completion can start a subsequent run after
  the reader reaccepts it; lower go_i unless repetition is intended.
- Hold runtime configuration stable before go and through the complete busy tail.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 06. Shared read data and multiplier sampling
// Source: fft_accelerator.sv
"
    assign {sample_real, sample_imag} = samples_rd_data_i;
    assign {twiddle_real, twiddle_imag} = twiddles_rd_data_i;
    fft_multiplier_core #(
        .BASE(BASE),
        .EXPONENT_WIDTH(EXPONENT_WIDTH),
        .MANTISSA_WIDTH(MANTISSA_WIDTH),
        .REGSITERED_MULTIPLIER(REGSITERED_MULTIPLIER),
        .REGISTERED_NORMALIZE(REGISTERED_NORMALIZE),
        .LUTX(LUTX),
        .GRADE(GRADE)
    ) multiplier (
        .clk_i(clk_i), .rst_i(multiplier_rst_p),
        .wr_i(twiddle_real), .wi_i(twiddle_imag),
        .br_i(sample_real), .bi_i(sample_imag), .ar_i(sample_real), .ai_i(sample_imag),
        .w_b_valid_i(b_valid && w_valid && !reset_active),
        .wrbr_o(wrbr), .wibi_o(wibi), .wrbi_o(wrbi), .wibr_o(wibr),
        .ar_o(ar), .ai_o(ai), .valid_o(multiplier_valid)
    );
"
- Real components occupy the upper DATA_WIDTH bits; imaginary components occupy
  the lower bits. Both A and B ports use the same returned sample bus.
- Matching sample/twiddle READ_LATENCY makes B and W arrive together. The
  multiplier captures them on b_valid&&w_valid, and internally samples A on the
  following clock. The reader's a_valid is retained as an alignment observation.
- No extra wrapper data register is inserted into these connections.
- A synchronous memory captures the presented B command on the next edge; its
  data/valid return R clocks after presentation. The multiplier samples them
  one further clock later because validity itself is registered.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 07. Early multiplier valid into the adder
// Source: fft_accelerator.sv
"
    ) adder (
        .clk_i(clk_i), .rst_i(adder_rst_p), .valid_i(multiplier_valid && !reset_active),
        .wrbr_i(wrbr), .wibi_i(wibi), .wrbi_i(wrbi), .wibr_i(wibr), .ar_i(ar), .ai_i(ai),
        .y0r_o(y0r), .y0i_o(y0i), .y1r_o(y1r), .y1i_o(y1i), .valid_o(adder_valid)
    );
"
- The multiplier issues WrBr, WiBi, WrBi and WiBr on consecutive clocks.
- Its valid_o is the early WiBi/A strobe. The adder accepts WrBr/WiBi/Ar/Ai then,
  waits two clocks to issue Ti=WrBi+WiBr, and preserves the existing four-clock
  throughput. It does not require all multiplier outputs coherent at the early strobe.
- The adder computes Tr=WrBr-WiBi, Ti=WrBi+WiBr, then Y0=A+T and Y1=A-T.
- Reset qualification suppresses new adder input tokens during reset propagation.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 08. Address delay and output writer
// Source: fft_accelerator.sv
"
    // Delay the whole address stream: writer samples B then A on adjacent edges.
    logic write_enable;
    pipe #(.DATA_WIDTH(SAMPLES_ADDR_WIDTH), .LATENCY(ADDRESS_PIPE_LATENCY)) address_pipe (
        .clk_i(clk_i), .data_i(read_addr), .data_o(write_addr_p)
    );
    fsm_fft_writer #(.DATA_WIDTH(COMPLEX_DATA_WIDTH), .ADDR_WIDTH(SAMPLES_ADDR_WIDTH)) writer (
        .clk_i(clk_i), .rst_i(writer_rst_p), .valid_i(adder_valid && !reset_active),
        .a_wr_addr_i(write_addr_p), .b_wr_addr_i(write_addr_p),
        .y0_i({y0r, y0i}), .y1_i({y1r, y1i}),
        .wr_addr_o(samples_wr_addr_o), .wr_data_o(samples_wr_data_o), .wr_en_o(write_enable)
    );
    assign samples_wr_en_o = write_enable && !reset_active;
"
- One delayed copy of the complete shared sample-address stream supplies both
  writer address inputs. B appears first and A appears one clock later.
- The writer samples B with the completed adder bundle, then samples A on the
  next edge. It captures Y0 early, preventing the next bundle's Y0r update from
  corrupting the second write at minimum core throughput.
- Y1 writes back to B and Y0 writes back to A. Both complex fields use the same
  memory-word packing as input. Consecutive high write enables mean two writes.
- Invalid/gap addresses may flow through the unreset address pipe; only valid
  writer commands enable memory, so those addresses are not consumed as writes.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 09. Clock-by-clock latency derivation
- Define C as the edge after which a B/W read command is presented, R as common
  READ_LATENCY, Lm as fft_multiplier_core LATENCY and La as fft_adder_core LATENCY.

   Event                              Edge
   B/W command presented              C
   A command presented                C+1
   Returned B/W data and reader valid  C+R
   Multiplier accepts B/W             C+R+1
   Adder accepts early product strobe  C+R+Lm+2
   Writer accepts completed result    C+R+Lm+La+3
   B/Y1 write command presented        C+R+Lm+La+3
   A/Y0 write command presented        C+R+Lm+La+4
   A/Y0 committed by external memory   C+R+Lm+La+4+WRITE_LATENCY

- ADDRESS_PIPE_LATENCY=R+Lm+La+2 puts B at the writer input before acceptance,
  and A before the next edge. LATENCY is the B-read to B-write presentation delay.
- WRITE_COMMIT_LATENCY reaches the final A write's commitment. These formulas
  include the one-clock handoffs between independently registered child modules.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 10. Busy tail and completion
// Source: fft_accelerator.sv
"
    generate
        if(DRAIN_LATENCY == 0) begin : gen_completion_bypass
            assign busy_o = reader_busy && !reset_active;
            assign fft_done_o = reader_done && !reset_active;
        end else begin : gen_completion_pipe
            logic [DRAIN_LATENCY - 1 : 0] busy_tail, done_tail;
            always @(posedge clk_i) begin
                if(control_rst_p) begin
                    busy_tail <= '0;
                    done_tail <= '0;
                end else begin
                    busy_tail[0] <= reader_busy;
                    done_tail[0] <= reader_done;
                    for(int i = 1; i < DRAIN_LATENCY; i++) begin
                        busy_tail[i] <= busy_tail[i - 1];
                        done_tail[i] <= done_tail[i - 1];
                    end
                end
            end
            assign busy_o = (reader_busy || (|busy_tail)) && !reset_active;
            assign fft_done_o = done_tail[DRAIN_LATENCY - 1] && !reset_active;
        end
    endgenerate
"
- Reader busy/done describe request generation. Top-level busy/done additionally
  account for the final memory-write commitment.
- Reader done is II_CYCLES-1 clocks after its last B request. DRAIN_LATENCY is
  max(0,WRITE_COMMIT_LATENCY-(II_CYCLES-1)). The delayed done therefore cannot
  precede the final write, including external write-input pipeline stages.
- Busy remains high while reader busy or any busy-tail token is high. Tail inputs
  come from reader_busy, not top-level busy, so there is no self-sustaining loop.
- When no drain is needed, the generated bypass forwards reader busy/done.
- All busy/done tokens reset synchronously from the dedicated control reset;
  data pipelines need not be flushed for a whole arithmetic latency after reset.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 11. External memory and twiddle contract
- Provide a sample read port and an independently accepting sample write port.
  Fixed READ_LATENCY is common to samples and twiddles; WRITE_LATENCY describes
  when a presented write actually commits. There is no ready/backpressure input.
- Twiddle address k means W_N**k for the runtime N. A stored table for larger L
  must be externally rescaled to index k*(L/N), or repopulated for N.
- Only sample data is bit-reversed. Twiddle coefficients remain in natural k order.
- A memory read/write collision has no defined forwarding behavior. Loading or
  changing memory contents/configuration while busy violates the intended contract.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 12. In-place stage dependencies and reset limits
- The reader is intentionally request-only. This wrapper adds no stage barrier,
  dependency interlock or forwarding. Address/result alignment alone does not make
  arbitrary multi-stage in-place transforms safe at II_CYCLES=4.
- A conservative sufficient interval is II_CYCLES>=WRITE_COMMIT_LATENCY. Every
  butterfly then commits before the next butterfly's read is sampled. Smaller
  intervals require an independently demonstrated dependency-safe schedule/memory.
- The file TB rejects unsafe intervals for multi-stage in-place tests rather than
  silently comparing a stale-data FFT. Independent two-point blocks may use II=4.
- Hold raw reset high at least RESET_HOLD_CYCLES edges, then allow delayed reset
  and the reader input register to observe release before starting new work.
- Reset aborts pending work, but cannot undo writes already committed. A partial
  B-only update can remain in external memory; the caller must reload/restart as needed.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 13. File-driven scaffold and display workflow
- testbench/fft_accelerator_tb/test_files contains fixed-depth hexadecimal complex
  memories: 32768 samples, 16384 twiddles. Names encode exponent/mantissa widths.
- Supplied 6_25_samples_0/1 files contain two real MLII ECG windows from public
  MIT-BIH record 100, at 360 Hz, calibrated to mV with zero imaginary component.
  Source/license/calibration/order/hash metadata is recorded in JSON sidecars.
- Samples are bit-reversed over 32768 entries. The loader reconstructs natural
  indices and reorders each selected runtime block; it rescales the source twiddle
  table for smaller N. It verifies exact file depths and hexadecimal word widths.
- +SAMPLES_FILE, +TWIDDLES_FILE and +RESULT_FILE select filenames at run time;
  +FFT_POINTS/+FFT_COUNT select transforms without recompiling. Other plusargs
  document source ordering, source twiddle size, output directory and sampling rate.
- The reference uses the existing exact ABFP adder/multiplier golden models and
  independent mathematical DIT loops. Event traces avoid storing every idle clock.
- The scoreboard compares read addresses/deadlines, output words/addresses,
  commitment times, completion and the full final memory image bit-for-bit.
- result_files holds complete result memories and JSON run metadata. The Python
  utility in components/utilities restores natural input order, displays waveform,
  spectrum and floating-point FFT error, and can regenerate the fixtures.
- NumPy error plots are supplementary. Exact ABFP model comparison is the SV
  pass/fail criterion; IEEE/host-float arithmetic is not substituted for ABFP.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 14. Verification evidence
- Prior wrapper integration evidence: simulate/fft_accelerator_checks/summary.json
  records eight WSL configurations, 40 complete runs and 24 reset aborts. That
  revision used a shared reset pipe; arithmetic/address wiring is otherwise unchanged.
- Current dedicated-reset evidence: simulate/fft_accelerator_reset_checks/summary.json
  records WSL depths 0/3 and Questa depths 1/3, checking distinct reset connections,
  latency and reset-abort recovery with the existing arithmetic models.
- Current file-driven ABFP32 Questa smoke: N=128, count=1, BASE=16, E=6, M=25,
  R=1, W=1, II=47. All 896 reads, writes and commitments matched; done was at
  relative clock 21058, and all final 32K memory words matched the golden result.
- Evidence: simulate/fft_accelerator_scaffolds/questa.log and the selected result
  memory/JSON. The viewer also read this result and generated a headless plot.
- WSL Verilator file-driven ABFP32 N=32768,count=1 passed using real ECG file 0:
  245760 butterflies; 491520 reads, writes and commitments each; completion at
  clock 11550722. Every event and all 32768 final memory words matched bit-for-bit.
  Evidence: simulate/fft_accelerator_scaffolds/full32768.log and
  result_files/6_25_result_0.txt with its JSON sidecar. Wall time was about 42 s.
- The optimized TB keeps memory arrays in the top-level memory model. Queued
  loading/snapshots avoid full-array virtual-interface trigger scans each clock;
  filenames stay in class metadata, avoiding unsupported virtual-interface strings.
- WSL smaller/batched cases passed at N=128,count=1 and N=64,count=4; the second
  ECG window was also exercised. N=256,count=1 passed with read latency 3,
  write latency 2 and reset depth 3, checking 2048 reads/writes/commits each.
- A dense N=2,count=32 case passed at II=4 and reset depth 0, with 64 reads,
  writes and commitments and completion at clock 173. Memory-pipeline, dense,
  small, batch and full-size cases all passed after the memory-model optimization.
  Consolidated evidence: testbench/fft_accelerator_tb/simulate_regression_logs/results.json.
- Oversized FFT requests and incorrectly sized memory files were rejected as
  intended. Python checks verified all 65536 encoded ECG samples against raw
  ADC calibration, all 16384 twiddles, ordering, SHA-256s, RNE ties and zero rules.
- The full-size result viewer produced result_files/ecg_32768.png. Its comparison
  against NumPy FFT of the stored input gave relative L2 error 2.17560649e-7 and
  maximum absolute error 0.00070409663. These are supplementary floating-point
  metrics; the SV pass above uses exact ABFP models.
- The eight-block N=128,count=8 run processed 1024 ECG samples and passed:
  3584 butterflies, 7168 reads/writes/commitments each, completion at clock 168450.
  Evidence: testbench/fft_accelerator_tb/simulate_regression_logs/fft128_1024.log
  and result_files/6_25_result_fft128_1024.txt with its JSON sidecar.
- The viewer's --all-blocks option displays all processed samples, separate block
  spectra and complex-bin errors. --exclude-dc hides bin zero without changing
  result data or normalizing magnitudes; ecg_fft128_1024_no_dc.png shows this run.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 15. User review checklist
- Confirm complex packing, bit-reversed input requirement and natural twiddle order.
- Confirm all five reset pipelines are independent instances with equal depth.
- Confirm the B/W -> A sampling schedule and the two arithmetic handoff clocks.
- Confirm delayed shared addresses, B/Y1 then A/saved-Y0 writes and final commitment.
- Confirm busy/drain/go semantics, memory latency assumptions and reset hold.
- Confirm request-only scope and the in-place dependency restriction.
- Confirm selectable fixed-depth files, provenance, exact-model verification and
  result/viewer workflow. Review tags are signed at Marc103's explicit instruction
  on 11/15/26.
[Marc103 - 11/15/26]
